WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.032 --> 00:00:07.343
In 1868, John Muir traveled to the Yosemite Valley in the Sierra Nevada Mountains of California.

2
00:00:07.344 --> 00:00:12.564
When he saw it, he wrote, "No temple made with human hands can compare with Yosemite."

3
00:00:12.565 --> 00:00:18.617
Muir was a pioneer in the science of ecology—the study of living things and their environments.

4
00:00:18.618 --> 00:00:25.221
He was also one of the first people to promote "ecotourism"—a combination of "ecology" and "tourism."

5
00:00:25.222 --> 00:00:35.455
In 1892, he founded an environmental organization called the Sierra Club, with the following motto: "Explore, enjoy, and protect the planet."

6
00:00:35.456 --> 00:00:44.318
Muir knew how important natural wonders were to the human spirit, and he encouraged the government to protect untouched areas for future generations.

7
00:00:44.319 --> 00:00:49.205
But careless people can damage the wilderness they wish to experience.

8
00:00:49.206 --> 00:00:59.080
In Muir's beloved Yosemite, for instance, the enormous Wawona Tree was nearly two thousand years old when a tunnel big enough for a car was cut through its trunk.

9
00:00:59.081 --> 00:01:07.861
The tree died not even a century later; the weakened trunk couldn't support the weight of the snow on its branches after a storm in 1969.

10
00:01:07.862 --> 00:01:14.688
Since the Wawona Tree fell, however, many people have realized the impact their travels can have on the environment.

11
00:01:14.689 --> 00:01:24.181
Although tourism has existed ever since people have had free time and money to spend, during the 1980s, the idea of ecotourism finally took hold.

12
00:01:24.182 --> 00:01:33.331
Its goals are to provide experiences in nature that educate the public, protect the environment, and ensure the prosperity of indigenous people.

13
00:01:33.332 --> 00:01:39.877
Ecologists felt that if people had a better understanding of the planet, they would work harder to protect it.

14
00:01:39.878 --> 00:01:49.999
They encouraged activities that allow people to learn about a variety of ecosystems and experience wildlife up close—without damaging the environment.

15
00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:58.311
Before ecotourism, many tourists would flock to interesting or relaxing places without a thought about the consequences of their actions.

16
00:01:58.312 --> 00:02:07.304
For example, Cancún Island, a narrow strip of land off the eastern coast of Mexico, served as a nesting site for sea turtles and seabirds.

17
00:02:07.305 --> 00:02:16.737
But in the 1970s, a resort was built to attract vacationers. Roads and golf courses followed, and soon, luxury hotels lined the beach.

18
00:02:16.738 --> 00:02:21.419
Crowds packed the resort area, seriously affecting the island's environment.

19
00:02:21.420 --> 00:02:31.623
Garbage piled up, sewage and chemicals contaminated the local lagoon, and sunscreen from the bodies of thousands of swimmers began to destroy the nearby coral reef.

20
00:02:31.624 --> 00:02:39.286
Ecotourism, on the other hand, offers some exciting alternatives to lying on a beach with thousands of other vacationers.

21
00:02:39.287 --> 00:02:47.492
Ecotourists can experience the wilderness and help maintain it by clearing old hiking trails while nature guides lead them through the forest.

22
00:02:47.493 --> 00:02:57.261
Working and camping on the trails, visitors learn firsthand what the slogan "Leave nothing but footprints, take nothing but photographs" really means.

23
00:02:57.262 --> 00:03:08.140
Rafting is another great way to interact with nature while leaving no trace on the river ecosystem, and because no motor is used, rafting conserves energy too.

24
00:03:08.141 --> 00:03:15.903
Ecotourists can also learn about the environment and help to preserve fragile ecosystems by visiting the world's rain forests.

25
00:03:15.904 --> 00:03:23.020
The Central American country of Costa Rica, for example, features trams and walkways suspended in the jungle's canopy.

26
00:03:23.021 --> 00:03:31.856
Visitors can ride or walk among the treetops and see birds, large mammals, and plants that collect water for the creatures living high in the trees.

27
00:03:31.857 --> 00:03:39.841
The money generated by vacationers supports rain-forest conservation and allows activists to fight deforestation.

28
00:03:39.842 --> 00:03:46.656
Travelers looking for adventure can experience Africa's biodiversity and contribute to protecting its wildlife.

29
00:03:46.657 --> 00:03:52.998
While tracking gorillas in the jungles of Rwanda, tourists can also catch sight of lions and elephants.

30
00:03:52.999 --> 00:03:58.395
Ecotourism companies hire indigenous people to act as guides for the tourists.

31
00:03:58.396 --> 00:04:10.473
When locals make an income by guiding and educating tourists attracted by the wildlife, the members of the community are more likely to protect the habitat and less likely to resort to poaching.

32
00:04:10.474 --> 00:04:18.100
To get a unique view of the Central Serengeti in Tanzania, visitors can fly above the African plain in a hot-air balloon.

33
00:04:18.101 --> 00:04:29.692
The largest wildlife migration in the world takes place here, so this is the only place on the planet visitors can witness herds of thousands of wildebeests and zebras passing in search of new pastures.

34
00:04:29.693 --> 00:04:35.400
Most people would say shooting a photograph on a safari is much better than shooting an animal.

35
00:04:35.401 --> 00:04:45.386
These sightseeing safaris have become a booming business for local tour operators, and the money the tourists bring in helps maintain the natural beauty of the area.

36
00:04:45.387 --> 00:04:55.212
The tourism industry has taken notice that as people become more environmentally conscious, they are willing to pay higher prices to vacation at "green" resorts.

37
00:04:55.213 --> 00:04:58.292
These places make the environment a priority.

38
00:04:58.293 --> 00:05:07.101
They try to use renewable resources, to conserve water, and to minimize their "carbon footprint"—the amount of greenhouse gases they produce.

39
00:05:07.102 --> 00:05:15.092
They may do this by using materials that can be replenished quickly, such as bamboo, instead of trees that take longer to grow back.

40
00:05:15.093 --> 00:05:23.195
Or they may use less water by giving guests the option of having their towels and sheets laundered every few days instead of daily.

41
00:05:23.196 --> 00:05:28.020
These practices also allow businesses to lower their energy consumption.

42
00:05:28.021 --> 00:05:33.574
By lessening their impact on the environment, resort owners can also increase their profits.

43
00:05:33.575 --> 00:05:39.685
Not only do their costs decrease, but green practices attract greater numbers of guests.

44
00:05:39.686 --> 00:05:46.689
Some unethical companies, however, take advantage of the public's willingness to spend a little extra to protect the planet.

45
00:05:46.690 --> 00:05:55.610
These companies mislead consumers by claiming to be green, even though their concern for the environment takes a back seat to their desire for profits.

46
00:05:55.611 --> 00:05:57.721
This is called "greenwashing."

47
00:05:57.722 --> 00:06:06.269
For example, in 2002, nature tourists paid to travel to a beach on the west coast of Africa to see sea turtles laying their eggs.

48
00:06:06.270 --> 00:06:15.772
Local activists watched with horror as the visitors drove along the beach on all-terrain vehicles, running over nests and rushing right up to the turtles.

49
00:06:15.773 --> 00:06:24.141
A spokeswoman for the International Fund for Animal Welfare criticized the tour's organizers, saying, "It was completely irresponsible.

50
00:06:24.142 --> 00:06:26.713
They didn't care about the animals."

51
00:06:26.714 --> 00:06:31.984
Many people do not understand the magnitude of humans' effect on sensitive habitats.

52
00:06:31.985 --> 00:06:37.920
But some people are aware of the dangers and are anxious to see the frontiers that are disappearing.

53
00:06:37.921 --> 00:06:48.734
On vacation, they travel to faraway places like Antarctica or Australia's Great Barrier Reef to see them before they become damaged beyond repair by global warming.

54
00:06:48.735 --> 00:06:52.402
Unfortunately, these trips often add to the problem.

55
00:06:52.403 --> 00:06:59.053
For instance, airplanes release large amounts of greenhouse gases and make global warming worse.

56
00:06:59.054 --> 00:07:02.560
Ecotourism has been a double-edged sword for the environment.

57
00:07:02.561 --> 00:07:10.735
On one hand, it has provided much-needed funds for the protection of endangered species and at-risk areas while educating the public.

58
00:07:10.736 --> 00:07:20.650
On the other hand, ecotourism has created a stampede into vulnerable areas of the world, hastening the destruction of many of these precious sites.

59
00:07:20.651 --> 00:07:29.312
Many hope that in the future, ecotourism will create even more chances for people to learn about nature, but in a more responsible way.

60
00:07:29.313 --> 00:07:36.849
Because the more people recognize how connected they are to the natural world, the more determined they will be to protect it.

